COIS- Declarations. Competing interests: The authors declare no competing interests. 
      Ethical and regulatory compliance: The present study did not involve direct 
      recruitment of human participants. Experimental validation was conducted using 
      (i) the MIT-BIH Arrhythmia database, a publicly available and fully de-identified 
      dataset that is exempt from Institutional Review Board (IRB) approval, and (ii) a 
      synthetically generated clinical dataset designed to simulate real-time sensor 
      data. As such, no additional ethical approval or patient consent was required for 
      the analyses reported. All data handling and system design were carried out in 
      accordance with Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA) and 
      General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) guidelines, ensuring that privacy, 
      confidentiality, and security principles were maintained throughout the workflow. 
      For future validation on real-world, multi-institutional cohorts, formal approval 
      will be obtained from the respective Institutional Review Boards (IRBs) or Ethics 
      Committees, and informed consent will be sought from participants or their legal 
      guardians. This forward-looking compliance strategy ensures that the proposed 
      framework remains aligned with both current standards and evolving regulatory 
      expectations in clinical research.
